# Secondary Vampire

## Lore

A master vampire creates other vampires with the slow kill, a bite that passes a
fragment of the vampire intelligence''s evil essence into its victim. Now that the
intelligence has anchored itself in the world through the master, it no longer
needs willing subjects: the master can turn willing and unwilling victims alike,
and every new vampire strengthens the alien intelligence''s hold on that world.

Those victims are the secondary vampires. They are usually subservient to their
creator and the intelligence, but they possess all the spectacular supernatural
powers of the master and are quite intelligent. Only two things make them lesser:
they are slightly less intelligent, and they are susceptible to their master''s
mind control. Perhaps because they hold a smaller fragment of the essence, or
because they answer to two masters, they sometimes defy the intelligence, and they
remember more of their human lives than any other undead.

Evil secondary vampires accept their monstrous existence readily. Those who were
good often loathe what they have become; most eventually succumb to the craving for
blood, but a tiny minority win the inner battle and keep some vestige of humanity.
They are the unprincipled, anarchist and aberrant vampires who operate apart from
any master, seldom feed indiscriminately on the innocent, and try to do some good
with their lives.

Vampires are a third front in the war between the Nightbane and the Nightlords.
The Ba''al hate vampires and hunt them, and vampires fight the Nightlords tooth and
nail, but most vampires see humans as cattle, which makes any lasting alliance with
the Nightbane nearly impossible.

## GM Notes

Because they remember more of their past, secondary vampires often choose family,
friends and loved ones as victims of the slow kill: the love is gone, but the shadow
of the memory compels them. Their slow kill is unreliable - a new secondary vampire
only 03-44% of the time.

Secondary vampires prefer their natural powers and instincts to technology. At most
one will drive a car or, very rarely, use a gun or other weapon. All vampires take
pleasure in using their supernatural powers.

As NPCs they are 2nd to 7th level (1D6+1); player characters start at first level.

Vampires sense their own kin. A vampire''s standing in the undead hierarchy is often
settled by mind control, and a vampire may acknowledge a leader only after being
dominated. Vampires of another intelligence are recognized as rivals and enemies.
